var e=`{{price}}`,t=`Removed from the row, after the approvals you set. Live for GitHub today; other apps as each connector switches on.`,n=`A frozen snapshot, decisions, named sign-off and an evidence pack: one CSV per app, with file hashes.`,r=`The free scan on your own GitHub org, with names hidden, and the live demo. Paid plans unlock names and fixes.`,i=[{slug:`lumos`,name:`Lumos`,title:`Truehound vs Lumos`,description:`Lumos governs human, machine and agent identities for larger companies. Truehound is a flat-priced GitHub audit that removes and proves. A sourced comparison.`,lede:`Lumos is an enterprise identity governance platform with a long feature list. Truehound is much narrower: it audits who still has access, removes it, and checks again.`,shortAnswer:`Choose Lumos if you are a larger company that needs identity governance across people, service accounts and agents, with just-in-time access and lifecycle automation. Choose Truehound if you are a small team that wants a flat public price, a free scan on your own GitHub org, and leavers removed with proof.`,theyAreStronger:[`A much broader platform: access reviews, just-in-time access, joiner, mover and leaver automation, role mining and governance of non-human identities.`,`Lists integrations in the hundreds (it says 300+) and a connector SDK. Truehound connects far fewer apps, and says which.`,`Request routes beyond the web app: Slack, IT systems and MCP, with policy checks and automatic revoke on a schedule.`,`Explains cryptic permissions in plain language for approvers, and sorts obvious access from judgment calls in its reviews.`,`Built for enterprise buyers, with customer names and public product tours. Truehound is new.`],weAreDifferent:[`The price is on the website, flat and monthly, with no minimum contract.`,`It starts with a scan of your own GitHub org that you can run before talking to anyone.`,`After removing access it reads the app again and shows anything still present, never as done.`,`GitHub depth: outside collaborators, deploy keys, webhooks, public repos and secrets in code are their own findings.`],rows:[{topic:`Price`,them:`No public price; the pricing page is an assessment form. Its AWS Marketplace listing shows a 12-month Core Platform contract covering 250 identities.`,themSources:[`lumos-pricing`,`lumos-aws`],us:e,usStatus:`live`},{topic:`Access reviews`,them:`A review agent scopes reviews, certifies the obvious access, investigates ambiguous items and assembles audit reports.`,themSources:[`lumos-reviews`],us:n,usStatus:`coming`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Removing access that fails review`,them:`Closes the loop with automation, and removes stale access and terminated accounts per its product pages.`,themSources:[`lumos-reviews`,`lumos-home`],us:t,usStatus:`partial`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Leavers`,them:`Driven by your HR system: leavers are offboarded and licenses reclaimed automatically.`,themSources:[`lumos-home`],us:`You tell it who left today (GitHub). With Google Workspace or Entra connected, a suspension starts the check across every connected app.`,usStatus:`partial`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Getting started`,them:`An identity assessment through a form, plus public product tours.`,themSources:[`lumos-pricing`,`lumos-tours`],us:r,usStatus:`live`}],faq:[{q:`Is Lumos built for small companies?`,a:`It is aimed at larger organizations: its customers are enterprises and its AWS Marketplace listing is a 12-month contract covering 250 identities. Truehound is built for small teams.`},{q:`Does Truehound do just-in-time access or role mining?`,a:`No. Truehound audits and removes existing access and keeps the proof. Lumos is the stronger choice for just-in-time access and role design.`}],sources:[{id:`lumos-home`,label:`Lumos: Home`,url:`https://www.lumos.com/`},{id:`lumos-pricing`,label:`Lumos: Pricing`,url:`https://www.lumos.com/pricing`},{id:`lumos-reviews`,label:`Lumos: Access reviews`,url:`https://www.lumos.com/products/access-reviews`},{id:`lumos-aws`,label:`AWS Marketplace: Lumos listing`,url:`https://aws.amazon.com/marketplace/pp/prodview-g7fmrspbd3c2i`},{id:`lumos-tours`,label:`Lumos: Product tours`,url:`https://www.lumos.com/product-tours`}]},{slug:`c1`,name:`C1`,title:`Truehound vs C1`,description:`C1 (formerly ConductorOne) is an identity platform for security teams at larger companies. Truehound is a small, flat-priced access audit. A sourced, fair comparison.`,lede:`C1 is a full identity platform for security and IAM teams. Truehound does one smaller job: find who still has access, remove it, and prove it.`,shortAnswer:`Choose C1 if you have a security or IAM team and need access requests, lifecycle, access reviews and identity security in one platform, with hundreds of integrations. Choose Truehound if you are small, want a flat public price you can pay by card, and mainly need leavers and stale access removed with proof.`,theyAreStronger:[`A whole identity platform: lifecycle, access requests, just-in-time access, access reviews, identity security and governance of agents.`,`Recurring review campaigns with templates, auto start and end, scoping by risk level or compliance framework, and revoking inherited access through groups.`,`A public API, a Terraform provider and an open-source connector SDK.`,`SOC 2 Type 2, ISO 27001 and HIPAA listed on its trust center. Truehound holds none of these.`,`A larger integration catalog: its site has hundreds of integration pages. Truehound connects far fewer apps.`],weAreDifferent:[`A flat public price instead of a quote or a usage meter.`,`No sales process: run the free scan on your own GitHub org, then pay by card.`,`After removing access it reads the app again and keeps the before and after.`,`GitHub depth: outside collaborators, deploy keys, webhooks, public repos and secrets in code are their own findings.`],rows:[{topic:`Price`,them:`No public numbers. You buy by platform (program size from 100 managed identities) or by usage (Flex), and exact prices come from sales.`,themSources:[`c1-pricing`],us:e,usStatus:`live`},{topic:`Access reviews`,them:`C1 Comply: recurring campaigns, scoping, evidence reports and remediation, bought as a module.`,themSources:[`c1-pricing`,`c1-release`],us:n,usStatus:`coming`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Removing access that fails review`,them:`Remediation is part of the review module, including revoking other paths to the same access.`,themSources:[`c1-release`],us:t,usStatus:`partial`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Getting started`,them:`Sales-led. The pricing page is a configurator, and support tiers are sized by company headcount.`,themSources:[`c1-pricing`,`c1-support`],us:r,usStatus:`live`},{topic:`Identity platform (requests, lifecycle, just-in-time access)`,them:`Yes. It is the core of the product.`,themSources:[`c1-home`],us:`No. Truehound audits and removes access; it does not run requests or provisioning.`,usStatus:`no`}],faq:[{q:`Is C1 the same as ConductorOne?`,a:`Yes: ConductorOne now uses the C1 name, per its own site.`},{q:`Does C1 publish its prices?`,a:`Its pricing page is a configurator without numbers; exact prices come from sales, checked on the date below.`}],sources:[{id:`c1-home`,label:`C1: Home`,url:`https://www.c1.ai/`},{id:`c1-pricing`,label:`C1: Pricing`,url:`https://www.c1.ai/pricing`},{id:`c1-support`,label:`C1: Support tiers`,url:`https://www.c1.ai/pricing/support`},{id:`c1-release`,label:`C1: Product release notes`,url:`https://www.c1.ai/docs/product/release-notes`}]},{slug:`zluri`,name:`Zluri`,title:`Truehound vs Zluri`,description:`Zluri combines SaaS management with identity governance for larger companies. Truehound is a flat-priced access audit that removes and proves. A sourced, fair comparison.`,lede:`Zluri covers SaaS discovery, spend and identity governance, and is now part of Infotech. Truehound is far narrower: who still has access, removed, and proved.`,shortAnswer:`Choose Zluri if you also want SaaS discovery, renewals and spend control alongside access reviews, with segregation of duties rules and integrations it puts at 300+. Choose Truehound if access is the whole job, you want a public flat price, and you want every removal checked again.`,theyAreStronger:[`SaaS management: app discovery, contracts, spend and license optimization, which Truehound does not do.`,`Segregation of duties rules, access requests, time-bound access and contractor access, beyond offboarding.`,`Integrations it puts at 300+, with interface automation for systems that have no API.`,`Review insights from usage activity, and one-click remediation in its reviews.`,`Dozens of public interactive product tours. Truehound has a live demo instead.`],weAreDifferent:[`The price is public, flat and monthly. Zluri sells by demo.`,`It starts with a scan of your own GitHub org that you can run before talking to anyone.`,`After removing access it reads the app again and shows anything still present, never as done.`,`GitHub depth: outside collaborators, deploy keys, webhooks, public repos and secrets in code are their own findings.`],rows:[{topic:`Price`,them:`No public price; sold by demo. Its AWS Marketplace listing shows a Base Plan billed by number of users for 12 months.`,themSources:[`zluri-aws`],us:e,usStatus:`live`},{topic:`Access reviews`,them:`Reviews with usage insights, auto-remediation and audit-ready reports, plus per-app review pages.`,themSources:[`zluri-reviews`],us:n,usStatus:`coming`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Leavers`,them:`Secure deprovisioning with granular steps such as transferring data, plus revocation beyond SCIM.`,themSources:[`zluri-deprov`],us:t,usStatus:`partial`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`SaaS spend and discovery`,them:`Yes: app discovery, contracts, renewals and license cost optimization.`,themSources:[`zluri-ivip`],us:`No. Truehound does not manage spend or discover apps people signed up for.`,usStatus:`no`},{topic:`Getting started`,them:`Demo-led; no trial found, but many public interactive product tours.`,themSources:[`zluri-tours`],us:r,usStatus:`live`}],faq:[{q:`Is Zluri a direct Truehound competitor?`,a:`Partly. Zluri is a SaaS management and identity platform; the overlap is access reviews and offboarding. For spend and discovery it is the stronger choice.`},{q:`What changed at Zluri recently?`,a:`Infotech completed its acquisition of Zluri on 2026-10-14, per Infotech's press release and the banner on Zluri's site.`}],sources:[{id:`zluri-ivip`,label:`Zluri: Identity visibility and intelligence`,url:`https://www.zluri.com/products/identity-visibility-and-intelligence`},{id:`zluri-reviews`,label:`Zluri: Access reviews`,url:`https://www.zluri.com/access-reviews`},{id:`zluri-deprov`,label:`Zluri: Secure deprovisioning`,url:`https://www.zluri.com/features/secure-deprovisioning`},{id:`zluri-aws`,label:`AWS Marketplace: Zluri listing`,url:`https://aws.amazon.com/marketplace/pp/prodview-thzrlnakkww5k`},{id:`zluri-tours`,label:`Zluri: Product tours`,url:`https://www.zluri.com/product-tours`}]},{slug:`torii`,name:`Torii`,title:`Truehound vs Torii`,description:`Torii manages SaaS apps, spend and identity governance, and is being acquired by Freshworks. Truehound audits access and proves removal. A sourced, fair comparison.`,lede:`Torii is a SaaS management platform first, with identity governance added. Truehound is not that: it audits who still has access and checks every removal.`,shortAnswer:`Choose Torii if your problem is knowing which SaaS apps you pay for and who uses them, with license reclamation, renewals and workflows. Choose Truehound if you need leavers and stale access removed, with proof, at a flat public price.`,theyAreStronger:[`SaaS management: discovery from SSO, finance and a browser extension, license reclamation, contracts and renewals.`,`A large integration catalog: its site has hundreds of integration pages, with new ones added weekly.`,`Request and approval flows for apps, and onboarding and offboarding workflows.`,`A privileged access view across apps, and access governance policies that flag deviations.`,`SOC 2 Type II listed on its security page. Truehound holds no SOC 2 report.`],weAreDifferent:[`The price is public, flat and monthly. Torii prices to your environment.`,`It reads each app again after removing access, so the proof is the app's own answer.`,`It starts with a scan of your own GitHub org that you can run before talking to anyone.`,`GitHub depth: outside collaborators, deploy keys, webhooks, public repos and secrets in code are their own findings.`],rows:[{topic:`Price`,them:`No public price; modules are priced to your environment. Its AWS Marketplace listing shows a Core Platform 12-month contract by private offer.`,themSources:[`torii-pricing`,`torii-aws`],us:e,usStatus:`live`},{topic:`Access reviews`,them:`Automated certifications with remediation, alongside access governance policies.`,themSources:[`torii-updates`],us:n,usStatus:`coming`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Leavers`,them:`Offboarding workflows with per-app methods. Its October 2026 update lets an admin mark an offboarding done to close the remaining apps and tasks.`,themSources:[`torii-updates`],us:`Removes access in the apps it connects, then re-reads each one. A manual removal is labelled manual, never verified.`,usStatus:`partial`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`SaaS spend and discovery`,them:`Yes: discovery, license reclamation, renewals and cost allocation.`,themSources:[`torii-home`],us:`No. Truehound does not manage spend or discover apps people signed up for.`,usStatus:`no`},{topic:`Ownership`,them:`Freshworks agreed to acquire Torii on 2026-10-12, to extend Freshservice.`,themSources:[`torii-freshworks`],us:`Independent and small. Truehound is new.`,usStatus:`live`}],faq:[{q:`Can Torii replace Truehound?`,a:`For SaaS discovery, spend and workflows, Torii is the stronger tool. Truehound is for the narrower job of finding and removing access and keeping the proof.`},{q:`Does Torii publish its prices?`,a:`Its pricing page lists three products priced to your environment, without numbers, checked on the date below.`}],sources:[{id:`torii-home`,label:`Torii: Home`,url:`https://www.toriihq.com/`},{id:`torii-pricing`,label:`Torii: Pricing`,url:`https://www.toriihq.com/pricing`},{id:`torii-updates`,label:`Torii: Product updates feed`,url:`https://product-updates.toriihq.com/api/public/rss.xml`},{id:`torii-aws`,label:`AWS Marketplace: Torii listing`,url:`https://aws.amazon.com/marketplace/pp/prodview-2ivpaavvbdtug`},{id:`torii-freshworks`,label:`Freshworks: Agreement to acquire Torii`,url:`https://www.freshworks.com/pressrelease/freshworks-to-extend-freshservice-into-ai-and-software-spend-governance-with-agreement-to-acquire-torii/`}]},{slug:`stitchflow`,name:`Stitchflow`,title:`Truehound vs Stitchflow`,description:`Stitchflow is a managed IT automation service that runs your offboarding. Truehound is self-serve, flat-priced and proves each removal. A sourced comparison.`,lede:`Stitchflow does the work for you as a managed service. Truehound is a product you run yourself, with a public price and a re-read after every removal.`,shortAnswer:`Choose Stitchflow if you want someone else to build and run your offboarding, license cleanup and access review workflows, including for apps with no API. Choose Truehound if you prefer self-serve, a flat public price, and a check of the app itself after each removal.`,theyAreStronger:[`A done-for-you service: it learns your process, builds the workflows and keeps them running.`,`Reaches apps without APIs through a local browser agent.`,`A clear offboarding receipt in Slack: users processed, apps covered, time elapsed and where the audit trail went.`,`Access reviews with reviewer sign-off in Slack, and evidence written back to Drata or Vanta.`,`SOC 2 Type II listed on its security page. Truehound holds no SOC 2 report.`],weAreDifferent:[`Self-serve: run the free scan on your own GitHub org and pay by card, with no services engagement.`,`The price is public, flat and monthly.`,`After removing access it reads the app again and shows anything still present, never as done.`,`GitHub depth: outside collaborators, deploy keys, webhooks, public repos and secrets in code are their own findings.`],rows:[{topic:`Price`,them:`Quote-only: its pricing page shows no numbers and says you test before you pay. Managed SCIM is priced per app.`,themSources:[`sf-pricing`,`sf-compare`],us:e,usStatus:`live`},{topic:`Offboarding`,them:`Workflows built per customer, covering API apps and browser-driven apps, with a completion card in Slack.`,themSources:[`sf-home`,`sf-how`],us:t,usStatus:`partial`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Access reviews`,them:`Continuous gap detection, reviewer sign-off in Slack and SLA enforcement.`,themSources:[`sf-home`],us:n,usStatus:`coming`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Apps without an API`,them:`A local browser agent works in a dedicated Chromium on your machine.`,themSources:[`sf-how`],us:`Not covered. Where no API exists, Truehound gives a deep link and labels the removal manual.`,usStatus:`no`},{topic:`Getting started`,them:`Sales-led. No billing until the first workflow runs in production; it offers a deprovisioning gap checker without signup.`,themSources:[`sf-pricing`,`sf-tools`],us:r,usStatus:`live`}],faq:[{q:`Is Stitchflow software or a service?`,a:`Its own site calls it a managed IT automation service: it builds the workflows with you and keeps them running.`},{q:`Does Stitchflow publish a price?`,a:`Not on its pricing page, checked on the date below. It says billing starts only after the first workflow runs in production.`}],sources:[{id:`sf-home`,label:`Stitchflow: Home`,url:`https://www.stitchflow.com/`},{id:`sf-how`,label:`Stitchflow: How it works`,url:`https://www.stitchflow.com/how-it-works`},{id:`sf-pricing`,label:`Stitchflow: Pricing`,url:`https://www.stitchflow.com/pricing`},{id:`sf-compare`,label:`Stitchflow: Stitchflow vs competitors`,url:`https://www.stitchflow.com/blog/stitchflow-vs-competitors`},{id:`sf-tools`,label:`Stitchflow: Tools`,url:`https://www.stitchflow.com/tools`}]},{slug:`cakewalk`,name:`Cakewalk`,title:`Truehound vs Cakewalk`,description:`Cakewalk now leads with access management for AI agents, with human access second. Truehound audits who still has access and proves removal. A sourced, fair comparison.`,lede:`Cakewalk has moved its focus to governing AI agents, and keeps a Slack-first human access product. Truehound stays on one job: who still has the keys, and proof they are gone.`,shortAnswer:`Choose Cakewalk if you want Slack-native access requests, no-touch onboarding from your HR system, and a gateway that governs what AI agents may do. Choose Truehound if you want a public flat price and a review that reads the apps themselves, then removes and re-checks.`,theyAreStronger:[`Access requests in Slack, onboarding from your HR system and time-based access, not only offboarding.`,`A runtime gateway for AI agents, with policy per tool call and an audit trail. Truehound has nothing like it.`,`An agent that creates and removes accounts in apps without SCIM.`,`ISO 27001:2022 and European-only data storage listed on its security page. Truehound holds no ISO 27001 certificate.`,`A public API, webhooks and an MCP server for the product.`],weAreDifferent:[`The price is public, flat and monthly, instead of a waitlist and per-unit marketplace pricing.`,`It reads each connected app again after removing access, so the proof is the app's own answer.`,`It starts with a scan of your own GitHub org that you can run before talking to anyone.`,`GitHub depth: outside collaborators, deploy keys, webhooks, public repos and secrets in code are their own findings.`],rows:[{topic:`Price`,them:`Quote-only: its pricing page showed no content when crawled and the home page asks for a waitlist signup. Its AWS Marketplace listing prices per unit for a contract term, without saying per what period.`,themSources:[`cw-pricing`,`cw-aws`],us:e,usStatus:`live`},{topic:`Access reviews`,them:`Manager, app owner or review owner as reviewer: approve, change or remove, with CSV export at any time.`,themSources:[`cw-home`],us:n,usStatus:`coming`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Removing access`,them:`An agent creates and removes accounts in apps without SCIM; tasks to app owners are the default for apps it does not manage.`,themSources:[`cw-agent-cake`,`cw-home`],us:t,usStatus:`partial`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Access requests and onboarding`,them:`Yes: Slack requests, bulk access in Slack and no-touch onboarding from your HR system.`,themSources:[`cw-changelog`],us:`No. Truehound audits and removes access; it does not run requests or onboarding.`,usStatus:`no`}],faq:[{q:`Is Cakewalk still a small-company access tool?`,a:`Its home page now leads with access management for AI agents, with the human access product second, checked on the date below.`},{q:`Does Cakewalk publish its prices?`,a:`Not on its own site as far as we could read it. The AWS Marketplace listing shows per-unit prices, but does not state the billing period.`}],sources:[{id:`cw-home`,label:`Cakewalk: Home`,url:`https://www.cakewalk.security/`},{id:`cw-pricing`,label:`Cakewalk: Pricing`,url:`https://www.cakewalk.security/pricing`},{id:`cw-aws`,label:`AWS Marketplace: Cakewalk listing`,url:`https://aws.amazon.com/marketplace/pp/prodview-osvhmolwtrwtk`},{id:`cw-changelog`,label:`Cakewalk: Changelog`,url:`https://www.cakewalk.security/docs/changelog`},{id:`cw-agent-cake`,label:`Cakewalk: Agent Cake`,url:`https://www.cakewalk.security/agent-cake`}]},{slug:`opal`,name:`Opal Security`,title:`Truehound vs Opal Security`,description:`Opal Security governs just-in-time and privileged access for engineering-heavy companies. Truehound is a flat-priced GitHub access audit. A sourced, fair comparison.`,lede:`Opal is built for infrastructure and privileged access at larger engineering companies, and now for AI agents. Truehound is smaller: an audit of who still has access, with proof.`,shortAnswer:`Choose Opal if you need just-in-time access to cloud and infrastructure, access reviews at scale and governance of AI agents, and you can buy through a demo. Choose Truehound if you are small, want a flat public price, and want leavers and stale access removed and re-checked.`,theyAreStronger:[`Just-in-time and break-glass access with time-bound grants that revoke themselves.`,`Strength in infrastructure: AWS, GCP, Entra roles, databases and Databricks, with integrations it puts at 250+.`,`Review campaigns with schedules, a snapshot at creation, and a reviewer preview.`,`A Terraform provider, CLI and REST API, and a self-hosted deployment option.`,`SOC 2 Type 2 listed on its platform page. Truehound holds no SOC 2 report.`],weAreDifferent:[`A flat monthly price rather than a price per user.`,`It starts with a scan of your own GitHub org that you can run before talking to anyone.`,`After removing access it reads the app again and shows anything still present, never as done.`,`GitHub depth: outside collaborators, deploy keys, webhooks, public repos and secrets in code are their own findings.`],rows:[{topic:`Price`,them:`The pricing page is a demo form. Its AWS Marketplace listing shows Opal Enterprise Platform at $240 per user per year.`,themSources:[`opal-pricing`,`opal-aws`],us:e,usStatus:`live`},{topic:`Access reviews`,them:`Campaigns with schedules, auto-stop when complete, a snapshot at creation and any revoke winning among several reviewers.`,themSources:[`opal-iga`,`opal-changelog`],us:n,usStatus:`coming`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Removing access`,them:`Time-bound grants are revoked automatically; reviews can revoke access in connected apps.`,themSources:[`opal-iga`],us:t,usStatus:`partial`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Just-in-time and privileged access`,them:`Yes. It is the core of the product.`,themSources:[`opal-platform`],us:`No. Truehound audits and removes standing access; it does not grant it.`,usStatus:`no`},{topic:`Getting started`,them:`Demo-led. No trial or free tools were found.`,themSources:[`opal-pricing`],us:r,usStatus:`live`}],faq:[{q:`Is Opal a direct Truehound competitor?`,a:`Only partly. Opal is aimed at larger engineering companies and increasingly at governing AI agents. The overlap is access reviews.`},{q:`What does Opal cost?`,a:`Its own pricing page is a demo form. The AWS Marketplace listing shows $240 per user per year, which we use for the cost table below.`}],cost:{plan:`Opal Enterprise Platform (AWS Marketplace list price)`,perUserMonthly:20,sources:[`opal-aws`],excludes:`This is the marketplace list price, $240 per user per year shown monthly. Negotiated contracts may differ.`},sources:[{id:`opal-platform`,label:`Opal: Platform`,url:`https://www.opal.dev/platform`},{id:`opal-pricing`,label:`Opal: Pricing`,url:`https://www.opal.dev/pricing`},{id:`opal-iga`,label:`Opal: AI-native IGA`,url:`https://www.opal.dev/ai-native-iga`},{id:`opal-changelog`,label:`Opal docs: Changelog`,url:`https://docs.opal.dev/changelog/changelog.md`},{id:`opal-aws`,label:`AWS Marketplace: Opal listing`,url:`https://aws.amazon.com/marketplace/pp/prodview-6xeygvxviq6bg`}]}],a=`{{price}}`,o=[{slug:`secureframe`,name:`Secureframe`,title:`Truehound vs Secureframe`,description:`Secureframe runs your compliance program, including access reviews on its Complete plan. Truehound takes back the access a review flags. A sourced comparison.`,lede:`Secureframe is a broad compliance platform with access reviews built in. Truehound is the narrow tool that removes the access and checks it is gone. Many teams on Secureframe will want to keep it.`,shortAnswer:`Choose Secureframe to run the whole compliance program: frameworks, policies, personnel tasks and an auditor view, with user access reviews on the Complete plan. Its offboarding tracks deprovisioning rather than performing it. Choose Truehound for the removal itself: it acts in the apps it connects, with your approval, and reads each app again to show it held.`,theyAreStronger:[`A full compliance platform: SOC 2, ISO 27001, HIPAA, PCI and a strong federal line (CMMC, FedRAMP, TX-RAMP). Truehound covers none of these frameworks.`,`More than 300 native integrations plus custom integrations. Truehound connects far fewer, and each connector card says what it can read and remove.`,`Access reviews with per-account decisions, designated reviewers per system, recurring schedules, reminders and an audit record of who decided what.`,`Personnel onboarding and offboarding tasks, policy acceptance, security training and a device agent in one place.`,`A public trust center with dated SOC 2 and ISO 27001 reports. Truehound holds no SOC 2 report.`],weAreDifferent:[`It removes access where a review decision becomes a task: GitHub members, outside collaborators and keys today, under the approval policy you set.`,`After removing, it reads the app again and shows anything still present in red, never as done.`,`The price is on the website and flat. Secureframe publishes no plan prices.`],rows:[{topic:`Price`,them:`Fundamentals, Complete and Defense packages with no public prices. Its AWS Marketplace listing shows $7,500 for 12 months for the platform plus one framework, up to 100 employees; access reviews need the Complete plan, which is not listed with a price.`,themSources:[`sf-pricing`,`sf-aws`],us:a,usStatus:`live`},{topic:`Access reviews`,them:`Generally available since March 2026 on the Complete plan: maintain, modify, revoke or out of scope per account, with recurring schedules and reminders.`,themSources:[`sf-uar`,`sf-updates`],us:`Snapshot, decisions, named sign-off and an evidence pack on the review plans.`,usStatus:`coming`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`When a reviewer revokes access`,them:`Changes become trackable tasks. Personnel deprovisioning is tracked and marked, not performed for you.`,themSources:[`sf-uar`,`sf-pricing`],us:`Removed from the row after the approvals you set. Live for GitHub today; other apps as each connector switches on.`,usStatus:`partial`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Proof the removal happened`,them:`An audit record of reviewers, decisions and how remediation was handled.`,themSources:[`sf-uar`],us:`Reads every connected app again after every removal and keeps the before and after.`,usStatus:`coming`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Compliance program (frameworks, policies, auditor view)`,them:`Yes. It is the core of the product.`,themSources:[`sf-pricing`],us:`No. Truehound does not run a compliance program and does not try to.`,usStatus:`no`}],useBoth:`Keep Secureframe to run the program and the review. Use Truehound where a decision to revoke has to turn into access actually gone, and you want the re-read as evidence.`,faq:[{q:`Does Secureframe have access reviews?`,a:`Yes. Its product updates list user access reviews as generally available in March 2026, on the Complete plan.`},{q:`Does Secureframe publish its prices?`,a:`Not on its own site. Its pricing page lists packages without numbers; its AWS Marketplace listing shows one entry price for the platform plus a first framework.`}],sources:[{id:`sf-pricing`,label:`Secureframe: Pricing`,url:`https://secureframe.com/pricing`},{id:`sf-uar`,label:`Secureframe: User access reviews`,url:`https://secureframe.com/features/user-access-reviews`},{id:`sf-updates`,label:`Secureframe: Product updates`,url:`https://secureframe.com/product-updates`},{id:`sf-aws`,label:`Secureframe on AWS Marketplace`,url:`https://aws.amazon.com/marketplace/pp/prodview-7cpg4l6nufwgq`},{id:`sf-trust`,label:`Secureframe: Trust center`,url:`https://trust.secureframe.com/`}]},{slug:`sprinto`,name:`Sprinto`,title:`Truehound vs Sprinto`,description:`Sprinto automates compliance for startups, with access reviews as one feature. Truehound removes the access and proves it. A sourced, fair comparison.`,lede:`Sprinto is a compliance automation platform for teams heading into a first audit. Truehound is the access slice: it removes what a review flags and checks it is gone.`,shortAnswer:`Choose Sprinto to get a first SOC 2 or ISO 27001 done: frameworks, evidence, policies and an auditor-ready trail, with user access reviews and an offboarding checklist inside. Choose Truehound for the removal itself, because in Sprinto a revoke creates a remediation task and offboarding is a checklist.`,theyAreStronger:[`A whole compliance platform: it lists 25+ frameworks automated, policies, training, a rule engine and a trust center product. Truehound does none of that.`,`Integrations it puts at more than 300. Truehound connects far fewer, and says which.`,`An access review table with bulk Mark as Okay, Revoke and Downgrade, plus recommended actions on flagged rows (February 2026).`,`Role-based and ticket-based access validation and login protection monitoring for critical systems.`,`Pricing that starts lower than the large platforms; its AWS Marketplace listing is a published number.`],weAreDifferent:[`It removes access where Sprinto opens a task: GitHub members, outside collaborators and keys today.`,`After removing, it reads the app again and shows anything still present in red.`,`One flat price on the website, not a platform price plus a charge per framework.`],rows:[{topic:`Price`,them:`Foundation and Growth plans are quoted by sales. Its AWS Marketplace listing shows a Starter Platform at $7,500 for 12 months, plus $2,000 for each core framework.`,themSources:[`sp-pricing`,`sp-aws`],us:a,usStatus:`live`},{topic:`Access reviews`,them:`User access review with remediation tasks; the review table supports bulk revoke and downgrade with recommended actions.`,themSources:[`sp-pricing`,`sp-feb`],us:`Snapshot, decisions, named sign-off and an evidence pack on the review plans.`,usStatus:`coming`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Offboarding`,them:`An access revocation checklist for offboarding, a checklist rather than automated removal.`,themSources:[`sp-pricing`],us:`Removal proposed for approval and carried out; live for GitHub today, other apps as each connector switches on.`,usStatus:`partial`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Proof the removal happened`,them:`Remediation tasks are tracked in the review; the pages we read do not describe re-reading the app.`,themSources:[`sp-feb`],us:`Reads every connected app again after every removal and keeps the before and after.`,usStatus:`coming`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Compliance program`,them:`Yes: frameworks, policies, training, evidence and a trust center.`,themSources:[`sp-pricing`],us:`No. Truehound does not run a compliance program.`,usStatus:`no`}],useBoth:`If Sprinto runs your audit, keep it. Add Truehound where you want the access it flags actually removed, with a re-read as proof.`,faq:[{q:`Does Sprinto remove access automatically?`,a:`Its pricing page lists an access revocation checklist for offboarding and a user access review with remediation tasks. We found no description of automated removal.`},{q:`Is Sprinto priced per user?`,a:`Its site quotes plans through sales. The AWS Marketplace listing shows a platform price plus a per-framework charge.`}],sources:[{id:`sp-pricing`,label:`Sprinto: Pricing`,url:`https://sprinto.com/pricing`},{id:`sp-feb`,label:`Sprinto docs: February 2026 product updates`,url:`https://docs.sprinto.com/product-updates/february-2026`},{id:`sp-aws`,label:`Sprinto on AWS Marketplace`,url:`https://aws.amazon.com/marketplace/pp/prodview-ixyb464cbjkam`},{id:`sp-trust`,label:`Sprinto: Trust center`,url:`https://trust.sprinto.com/`}]},{slug:`jumpcloud`,name:`JumpCloud`,title:`Truehound vs JumpCloud`,description:`JumpCloud is an identity, access and device platform. Truehound audits who still has the keys. A sourced, fair comparison, and why they work together.`,lede:`JumpCloud is the directory, SSO and device platform many small teams run on. Truehound is not a replacement: it checks what that directory and your apps still let in.`,shortAnswer:`Choose JumpCloud to run identity: a cloud directory, SSO, MFA, device management and user lifecycle, with a published per-user price. It is not an access review tool; none of the pages we read describe access reviews. Choose Truehound to audit who still has access and prove removals held. Many teams use JumpCloud as the identity source and Truehound on top.`,theyAreStronger:[`A full identity and device platform: directory, SSO, MFA, device management, cloud LDAP and RADIUS, password manager, privileged access.`,`Access requests and user lifecycle management inside the same product.`,`Published per-user prices and a 30-day trial.`,`A large integration directory with HR systems, Office 365, Slack and more.`,`Years in market and a wide partner and MSP program. Truehound is new.`],weAreDifferent:[`It starts from the question an auditor asks: who still has access, and was it removed. GitHub is live today.`,`After removing, it reads the app again and shows anything still present in red.`,`A flat price, not per user.`],rows:[{topic:`Price`,them:`Per user per month, billed annually: $9, $11 or $13 by package ($11, $13 or $15 monthly); add-ons from $3 to $5 per user. Larger platform packages are quoted by sales.`,themSources:[`jc-pricing`],us:a,usStatus:`live`},{topic:`Access reviews`,them:`No access review product page in the pages we read; we could not confirm whether SaaS Management includes one.`,themSources:[`jc-pricing`],us:`Snapshot, decisions, named sign-off and an evidence pack on the review plans.`,usStatus:`coming`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Who leaves and what gets removed`,them:`User lifecycle management and SCIM or SSO apps remove access in the apps it manages.`,themSources:[`jc-pricing`,`jc-integrations`],us:`Checks the apps around the directory too, GitHub today, and proposes removals for approval.`,usStatus:`partial`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`As a source`,them:`Directory sync and SSO apps make it a natural identity source.`,themSources:[`jc-integrations`],us:`Truehound reads JumpCloud as a source today.`,usStatus:`partial`,usDep:`sources`}],useBoth:`Run identity in JumpCloud and use Truehound to check what it does not reach, then prove the removal.`,faq:[{q:`Does JumpCloud do access reviews?`,a:`We found no access review product in the pages we read. Check with JumpCloud whether SaaS Management covers it.`},{q:`Is JumpCloud cheaper than Truehound?`,a:`It depends on headcount. JumpCloud charges per user; Truehound is one flat price. They also do different jobs.`}],cost:{plan:`JumpCloud top published package, billed annually`,perUserMonthly:13,sources:[`jc-pricing`],excludes:`Add-ons, sales-quoted platform packages and device hardware.`},sources:[{id:`jc-pricing`,label:`JumpCloud: Pricing`,url:`https://jumpcloud.com/pricing`},{id:`jc-integrations`,label:`JumpCloud: Integrations`,url:`https://jumpcloud.com/integrations`},{id:`jc-compliance`,label:`JumpCloud: Compliance use case`,url:`https://jumpcloud.com/use-cases/compliance`}]},{slug:`rippling`,name:`Rippling`,title:`Truehound vs Rippling`,description:`Rippling provisions and offboards apps from your HR record. Truehound checks what was missed and proves removals. A sourced, fair comparison.`,lede:`Rippling runs HR, payroll and IT on one employee record, so it provisions and offboards apps itself. Truehound is for the check afterward: what was missed, and proof it is gone.`,shortAnswer:`Choose Rippling if you want HR, payroll, devices and app access run from one record, with offboarding driven by the HR system. Choose Truehound if you want an independent check of who still has access, GitHub today, with a re-read after each removal. If you already run Rippling HR, Truehound can use it as the source of who left.`,theyAreStronger:[`One platform for HR, payroll, IT and finance, so a termination in HR drives app removal.`,`More than 600 pre-built app integrations with SSO and lifecycle provisioning.`,`Device management, a password manager and an IT helpdesk in the same suite.`,`A trust page listing SOC 1, SOC 2 and SOC 3 reports and ISO certifications. Truehound holds none.`,`A trial on its identity product.`],weAreDifferent:[`It is independent of the system doing the removal, so it can catch what that system missed.`,`After removing, it reads the app again and shows anything still present in red.`,`A flat price on the website, with no platform base fee.`],rows:[{topic:`Price`,them:`Plans start at $8 per employee per month with a monthly base fee, quoted for the actual product mix. IT products are add-ons whose prices we did not find published.`,themSources:[`rp-pricing`],us:a,usStatus:`live`},{topic:`Offboarding`,them:`Lifecycle provisioning from the HR record across its integrated apps, with advanced Microsoft 365 offboarding actions.`,themSources:[`rp-iam`,`rp-jan`],us:`A suspension or departure triggers a check of every connected app, GitHub today, and proposes removals for approval.`,usStatus:`partial`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Apps outside its catalog`,them:`Provisioning works through its pre-built integrations; we found no description of access reviews with evidence for apps outside them.`,themSources:[`rp-iam`],us:`Reads each connected app directly and keeps the before and after.`,usStatus:`coming`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Proof the removal happened`,them:`We found no description of re-reading an app to confirm a removal.`,themSources:[`rp-iam`],us:`Reads every connected app again after every removal.`,usStatus:`coming`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`HR, payroll and devices`,them:`Yes. It is the core of the suite.`,themSources:[`rp-pricing`],us:`No. Truehound does not run HR or payroll.`,usStatus:`no`}],useBoth:`Let Rippling provision and offboard. Use Truehound to check nothing was missed and keep the proof.`,faq:[{q:`Does Rippling remove access when someone is terminated?`,a:`For apps it integrates with, yes: its identity product provisions and offboards from the HR record.`},{q:`Can Truehound use Rippling?`,a:`Rippling HR as a source of who left is part of our plan and switches on with the connectors; GitHub is live today.`}],cost:{plan:`Rippling platform floor, before IT add-ons`,perUserMonthly:8,sources:[`rp-pricing`],excludes:`The monthly base fee and the IT products (identity, devices), which are quoted separately.`},sources:[{id:`rp-pricing`,label:`Rippling: Pricing`,url:`https://www.rippling.com/pricing`},{id:`rp-iam`,label:`Rippling: Identity and access management`,url:`https://www.rippling.com/identity-access-management`},{id:`rp-jan`,label:`Rippling: January 2026 product updates`,url:`https://www.rippling.com/blog/january-2026-product-updates`},{id:`rp-security`,label:`Rippling: Security`,url:`https://www.rippling.com/trust/security`}]},{slug:`bettercloud`,name:`BetterCloud`,title:`Truehound vs BetterCloud`,description:`BetterCloud automates SaaS management and Google Workspace admin. Truehound audits who still has access and proves removals. A sourced, fair comparison.`,lede:`BetterCloud is a SaaS management platform built on workflow automation. Truehound is narrower: it checks who still has access and shows the removal held.`,shortAnswer:`Choose BetterCloud for no-code workflow automation across your SaaS stack, deep Google Workspace administration and spend optimization. Choose Truehound if what you need is an access audit with evidence, starting with GitHub, at a flat price.`,theyAreStronger:[`No-code user automation with triggers from HR systems and actions across many apps.`,`Deep Google Workspace administration, including file governance for shared drives.`,`Spend optimization with license reclamation and app discovery.`,`AI governance discovery of API keys and agents, added in August 2026.`,`A $0 Spend Optimization Basic tier and a written ROI guarantee on its pricing page.`],weAreDifferent:[`It is an access audit, not an automation builder: the output is who still has access, removals, and a re-read as proof.`,`GitHub depth: outside collaborators, deploy keys and webhooks are their own findings.`,`One flat price on the website; BetterCloud modules are quoted.`],rows:[{topic:`Price`,them:`Modules (User Automation, Spend Optimization, Workspace Management for Google) are priced by quote. Spend Optimization Basic is listed at $0 a month.`,themSources:[`bc-pricing`],us:a,usStatus:`live`},{topic:`Offboarding`,them:`A workflow you build: HR triggers such as a changed termination date start actions across connected apps.`,themSources:[`bc-spring`],us:`A suspension or departure triggers a check of every connected app, GitHub today, with removals proposed for approval.`,usStatus:`partial`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Proof the removal happened`,them:`We found no description of re-reading an app to confirm a removal in the pages we read.`,themSources:[`bc-pricing`],us:`Reads every connected app again after every removal and keeps the before and after.`,usStatus:`coming`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Access reviews for an audit`,them:`Not the focus of the pages we read; it centers on automation, spend and governance.`,themSources:[`bc-pricing`,`bc-ai`],us:`Snapshot, decisions, named sign-off and an evidence pack on the review plans.`,usStatus:`coming`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Google Workspace administration`,them:`Granular controls, lifecycle automation and file governance.`,themSources:[`bc-spring`],us:`Google Workspace is read as a source of who left; we do not administer it.`,usStatus:`partial`,usDep:`sources`}],faq:[{q:`Is BetterCloud an access review tool?`,a:`Not mainly. Its pricing page presents workflow automation, spend optimization and Google Workspace management.`},{q:`Does BetterCloud publish prices?`,a:`Mostly no. Modules are quoted, apart from a $0 Spend Optimization Basic tier.`}],sources:[{id:`bc-pricing`,label:`BetterCloud: Pricing`,url:`https://www.bettercloud.com/pricing`},{id:`bc-spring`,label:`BetterCloud: What's new, Spring 2026`,url:`https://www.bettercloud.com/product-updates/whats-new-in-bettercloud-spring-2026`},{id:`bc-ai`,label:`BetterCloud: AI Governance arrives`,url:`https://www.bettercloud.com/product-updates/ai-governance-arrives`},{id:`bc-security`,label:`BetterCloud: Security and compliance`,url:`https://www.bettercloud.com/security-and-compliance`}]},{slug:`josys`,name:`Josys`,title:`Truehound vs Josys`,description:`Josys is an identity governance and SaaS management platform with access reviews. Truehound is a narrower GitHub-first access audit. A sourced, fair comparison.`,lede:`Josys is a broad identity governance platform with automated reviews and offboarding. Truehound is smaller and flat-priced, and it checks each removal by reading the app again.`,shortAnswer:`Choose Josys for a wide governance platform: identity and app discovery, access requests, scheduled access review surveys, leaked credential monitoring and lifecycle policies, quoted by number of identities. Choose Truehound for a self-serve, flat-priced access audit that starts with GitHub and proves each removal.`,theyAreStronger:[`Identity and app discovery from login data and browser activity, including non-human identities and AI agents.`,`Access reviews as mobile-friendly surveys with a question library, reminders and periodic schedules, ending in revocation.`,`Leaked credential detection matched to your identity inventory.`,`Access requests, a self-service portal and a policy engine for joiners, movers and leavers.`,`A trust page listing SOC 2 Type II and ISO 27001. Truehound holds neither.`],weAreDifferent:[`Self-serve: run the free scan in minutes and pay by card when you want names and fixes.`,`A flat price on the website; Josys prices by number of identities after a conversation.`,`After removing, it reads the app again and shows anything still present in red.`],rows:[{topic:`Price`,them:`One plan, priced by quote on the number of identities. A 14-day trial of the full platform is offered. We found no public number.`,themSources:[`jo-pricing`],us:a,usStatus:`live`},{topic:`Access reviews`,them:`Review surveys with a question library, Slack and Teams notifications, reminders and periodic schedules, with closed-loop revocation.`,themSources:[`jo-reviews`],us:`Snapshot, decisions, named sign-off and an evidence pack on the review plans.`,usStatus:`coming`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Offboarding`,them:`Policy-driven leaver removal across apps, including apps outside SSO.`,themSources:[`jo-lifecycle`],us:`Removal proposed for approval and carried out; live for GitHub today, other apps as each connector switches on.`,usStatus:`partial`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Integrations`,them:`Its pricing page says 350+ native integrations; its platform page says 1000+.`,themSources:[`jo-pricing`,`jo-platform`],us:`GitHub today. Google Workspace, Entra, Slack and more are built and switching on.`,usStatus:`partial`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Leaked credential monitoring`,them:`Yes, matched to the identity inventory with automated response.`,themSources:[`jo-threat`],us:`No.`,usStatus:`no`}],faq:[{q:`Does Josys do access reviews?`,a:`Yes. Its access review page describes scheduled survey-style reviews with reminders and revocation.`},{q:`Does Josys publish a price?`,a:`No public number. It prices by number of identities.`}],sources:[{id:`jo-pricing`,label:`Josys: Pricing`,url:`https://www.josys.com/pricing`},{id:`jo-reviews`,label:`Josys: Access reviews`,url:`https://www.josys.com/features/access-reviews`},{id:`jo-lifecycle`,label:`Josys: Access lifecycle automation`,url:`https://www.josys.com/features/access-lifecycle-automation`},{id:`jo-platform`,label:`Josys: Platform`,url:`https://www.josys.com/platform`},{id:`jo-threat`,label:`Josys: Identity threat monitoring`,url:`https://www.josys.com/features/identity-threat-monitoring`}]}],s=`{{price}}`,c=[{slug:`augmentt`,name:`Augmentt`,title:`Truehound vs Augmentt`,description:`Augmentt secures Microsoft 365 tenants for MSPs. Truehound audits who still has access to your GitHub. A sourced, fair comparison.`,lede:`Augmentt is a Microsoft 365 security platform sold to MSPs. Truehound is an access audit you run yourself, starting with GitHub. They overlap only when your IT is outsourced.`,shortAnswer:`Choose Augmentt if you are an MSP, or your MSP is, and the job is pushing security baselines across Microsoft 365 tenants. Choose Truehound if the question is who still has access to your code and apps after people leave, with removals you approve and a re-read that proves them.`,theyAreStronger:[`Multi-tenant management: baselines mapped to frameworks such as CIS, NIST and SCuBA applied across many client tenants, with drift detection.`,`Deep Microsoft 365 coverage: Entra ID, Exchange, Teams, SharePoint and OneDrive, plus login and breach alerts.`,`Branded client reports for MSPs, and PSA ticket integrations.`,`Device management templates through its Intune module. Truehound does not manage devices.`],weAreDifferent:[`It is built for the company that owns the access, not the MSP that manages many tenants.`,`It starts from GitHub: members, outside collaborators, keys and webhooks, none of which a Microsoft 365 baseline covers.`,`One flat price, not per seat through an MSP bill.`],rows:[{topic:`Price`,them:`Per seat, billed to the MSP, with modules sold separately and a quote required. Its pricing terms put $250 a month as the lowest entry price.`,themSources:[`augmentt-pricing`],us:s,usStatus:`live`},{topic:`Who it is for`,them:`MSPs only. Companies reach it through their provider.`,themSources:[`augmentt-home`,`augmentt-pricing`],us:`The company itself: self-serve, with a card and no provider in the middle.`,usStatus:`live`},{topic:`What it covers`,them:`Microsoft 365 security: baselines, drift, breach monitoring and remediation from a ticket, delegated through GDAP.`,themSources:[`augmentt-features`,`augmentt-integrations`],us:`GitHub access today. Google Workspace, Entra, Slack and AWS IAM are built and switching on.`,usStatus:`partial`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Trying it first`,them:`A risk assessment you can run on a prospect tenant before paying, aimed at MSP sales.`,themSources:[`augmentt-pricing`],us:`The free scan: run it on your own GitHub in minutes, names hidden until you pay.`,usStatus:`live`},{topic:`SaaS discovery`,them:`A separate Discover module finds apps clients run and unused licences.`,themSources:[`augmentt-features`],us:`No. Truehound audits access in connected apps; it does not find unknown apps.`,usStatus:`no`}],useBoth:`If an MSP runs your Microsoft 365, let it keep Augmentt for tenant security. Use Truehound for the GitHub side, which sits outside that scope.`,faq:[{q:`Can I buy Augmentt directly?`,a:`Its pricing page says subscriptions are billed to the MSP, so a company normally buys through its provider.`},{q:`Does Augmentt audit GitHub access?`,a:`Its published modules and integrations center on Microsoft 365 and PSA tools; we found no GitHub access audit on its site.`}],sources:[{id:`augmentt-home`,label:`Augmentt: Home`,url:`https://www.augmentt.com/`},{id:`augmentt-pricing`,label:`Augmentt: Pricing`,url:`https://www.augmentt.com/pricing`},{id:`augmentt-features`,label:`Augmentt: Platform features`,url:`https://www.augmentt.com/platform-features`},{id:`augmentt-integrations`,label:`Augmentt: Integrations`,url:`https://www.augmentt.com/integrations`}]},{slug:`corma`,name:`Corma`,title:`Truehound vs Corma`,description:`Corma governs SaaS licences, spend and access for European teams. Truehound audits who still has access and proves removal. A sourced, fair comparison.`,lede:`Corma is a wide SaaS management tool with a low per-user price and a $0 entry tier. Truehound does one job: find access that should be gone, remove it with approval, and prove it.`,shortAnswer:`Choose Corma if you want contracts, licence waste, shadow IT and provisioning in one place, with a very low per-user price. Choose Truehound if the job is the access review itself: a flat price that includes reviews, and a re-read of each app after every removal.`,theyAreStronger:[`Breadth: discovery, contracts and invoices, licence waste, provisioning, access requests and non-human identity visibility in one product.`,`Integrations it puts at over 200 on its Pro plan, plus browser agents for apps without an API.`,`A $0 entry tier and a low per-user price, with a startup discount.`,`OAuth token revocation, a workflow builder, EU data residency and a Gartner SaaS management recognition.`,`Named customers and a much longer track record. Truehound is new.`],weAreDifferent:[`Access reviews are in the price we publish. At Corma they sit in Pro, which is quoted.`,`Every removal is followed by a re-read of the app; anything still present shows in red, never as done.`,`GitHub depth: outside collaborators, deploy keys, webhooks and secrets in code are their own findings.`,`A flat price that does not grow with headcount.`],rows:[{topic:`Price`,them:`A $0 tier; Essential from 3 euros a user a month on annual terms; Pro by quote. Its AWS Marketplace listing shows Corma Advanced at $3.00 a user a month.`,themSources:[`corma-pricing`,`corma-aws`],us:s,usStatus:`live`},{topic:`Access reviews`,them:`Automated access reviews with PDF and CSV reports are listed in Pro, not in Essential.`,themSources:[`corma-pricing`],us:`Part of the review plans: snapshot, decisions, named sign-off and an evidence pack.`,usStatus:`coming`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Removing access`,them:`Automated provisioning and offboarding, and OAuth token revocation, including for apps without an API through browser agents.`,themSources:[`corma-pricing`,`corma-platform`],us:`Removed after the approvals you set, then read again. Live for GitHub today; other apps as each connector switches on.`,usStatus:`partial`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Integrations`,them:`200+ on Pro, drawing on identity provider logs, a browser extension, APIs and agents.`,themSources:[`corma-pricing`],us:`GitHub today. Google Workspace, Entra, Slack and AWS IAM are built and switching on.`,usStatus:`partial`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Contracts and licence spend`,them:`Central contract store, invoice scanning, unused seat detection and benchmarks.`,themSources:[`corma-platform`],us:`No. Truehound does not manage spend or contracts.`,usStatus:`no`}],useBoth:`Use Corma for the SaaS inventory, contracts and licence savings. Use Truehound where you need a sign-off-ready access review with proof of each removal.`,faq:[{q:`Is Corma cheaper than Truehound?`,a:`At 50 people its per-user price comes out below our flat price on paper. The comparison changes once you need access reviews, which its pricing table places in the quoted Pro plan.`},{q:`Does Corma remove access?`,a:`Yes: its platform pages describe automated provisioning, offboarding and token revocation. Truehound's difference is the read-back after each removal.`}],cost:{plan:`Corma Advanced (AWS Marketplace listing)`,perUserMonthly:3,sources:[`corma-aws`],excludes:`Corma's own Essential tier is 3 euros a user a month and does not include access reviews, which sit in the quoted Pro plan.`},sources:[{id:`corma-pricing`,label:`Corma: Pricing`,url:`https://www.corma.io/pricing`},{id:`corma-platform`,label:`Corma: Identity access management`,url:`https://www.corma.io/platform/identity-access-management`},{id:`corma-aws`,label:`AWS Marketplace: Corma listing`,url:`https://aws.amazon.com/marketplace/pp/prodview-nejyg3crxphnw`}]},{slug:`spendflo`,name:`Spendflo`,title:`Truehound vs Spendflo`,description:`Spendflo runs procurement and SaaS renewals. Truehound audits who still has access. They solve different problems; a sourced, fair comparison.`,lede:`Spendflo buys and renews software. Truehound checks who can still get into it. These are different jobs, and most teams would not choose between them.`,shortAnswer:`Choose Spendflo to run purchasing: intake, approvals, contracts, renewals and payments. Choose Truehound to find and remove access that should be gone. Spendflo's site now centers on procurement; we found no access review on it.`,theyAreStronger:[`Intake-to-pay procurement: request forms, multi-step approvals, purchase orders, invoice matching and payments.`,`Contract ingestion, renewal alerts and supplier risk scoring.`,`Agents for app approval and auto-renewal prevention.`,`A security page that lists SOC 2 Type II. Truehound holds no SOC 2 report.`],weAreDifferent:[`It looks at access, not spend: who can reach GitHub and, as connectors switch on, other apps.`,`Removals are approved by a person and read back to prove they held.`,`The price is public and flat, not a platform fee plus a fee per completed request.`],rows:[{topic:`Price`,them:`Quote only: a platform fee plus a fee per completed request, in Grow, Scale and Enterprise tiers.`,themSources:[`spendflo-pricing`],us:s,usStatus:`live`},{topic:`What it is for`,them:`Procurement and finance: intake, approvals, suppliers, contracts, purchase orders and invoices, with AI agents.`,themSources:[`spendflo-home`,`spendflo-pricing`],us:`Access: who has it, who should not, and proof the extra access is gone.`,usStatus:`live`},{topic:`Access reviews and removal`,them:`Not described on its pricing or product pages.`,themSources:[`spendflo-pricing`],us:`GitHub access audit live today; reviews and other apps behind switch-ons.`,usStatus:`partial`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Renewals and contracts`,them:`Renewal calendar with 30, 60 and 90 day alerts, plus contract review.`,themSources:[`spendflo-pricing`],us:`No. Truehound does not handle purchasing or contracts.`,usStatus:`no`}],useBoth:`Run buying and renewals in Spendflo. Run the access audit in Truehound, so the tools you buy do not keep access for people who left.`,faq:[{q:`Is Spendflo an access review tool?`,a:`Its current pricing and product pages describe procurement; we found no access review on them.`},{q:`Do I need both?`,a:`Only if you have both problems. They do not overlap, so neither replaces the other.`}],sources:[{id:`spendflo-home`,label:`Spendflo: Home`,url:`https://www.spendflo.com/`},{id:`spendflo-pricing`,label:`Spendflo: Pricing`,url:`https://www.spendflo.com/pricing`},{id:`spendflo-new`,label:`Spendflo: What's new`,url:`https://www.spendflo.com/whats-new`}]},{slug:`vendr`,name:`Vendr`,title:`Truehound vs Vendr`,description:`Vendr negotiates software prices and is now part of Vertice. Truehound audits who still has access. A sourced, fair comparison.`,lede:`Vendr helps you pay less for software. Truehound helps you find who can still get into it. Different jobs; this page says where Vendr is stronger.`,shortAnswer:`Choose Vendr (now part of Vertice) to benchmark and negotiate software contracts. Choose Truehound to find access that should be gone and remove it with proof. They do not compete.`,theyAreStronger:[`Pricing benchmarks drawn from a large set of negotiated software contracts.`,`Contract review and negotiation, with human approval before anything is sent.`,`Renewal routing, and a broader procurement platform since its acquisition by Vertice.`,`Zero-cost price checks on software quotes. Truehound has nothing like it.`],weAreDifferent:[`It audits access, not spend: who can reach your code and, as connectors switch on, other apps.`,`It removes access with your approval and reads each app again to prove it.`,`A flat price, published on the site.`],rows:[{topic:`Price`,them:`Vendr's own pricing page now redirects to Vertice and no price is shown there. Its AWS Marketplace listing shows 12-month packages of $15,000 and $20,000.`,themSources:[`vendr-home`,`vendr-aws`],us:s,usStatus:`live`},{topic:`What it is for`,them:`Software pricing intelligence and negotiation agents, part of Vertice since its June 2026 acquisition.`,themSources:[`vendr-home`,`vendr-news`],us:`Access audit and proof of removal.`,usStatus:`live`},{topic:`Access governance`,them:`None found on its site or in its marketplace listing.`,themSources:[`vendr-home`,`vendr-aws`],us:`GitHub access audit live today; reviews and other apps behind switch-ons.`,usStatus:`partial`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Contract negotiation`,them:`Yes. It is the core of the product.`,themSources:[`vendr-home`],us:`No. Truehound does not negotiate or buy.`,usStatus:`no`}],useBoth:`Use Vendr to pay less for tools. Use Truehound so the tools you pay for are not open to people who left.`,faq:[{q:`Is Vendr still independent?`,a:`Vertice announced its acquisition of Vendr on 1 June 2026, and Vendr's home page now says it is part of Vertice.`},{q:`Does Vendr review access?`,a:`We found no access review on its site. It is a buying tool.`}],sources:[{id:`vendr-home`,label:`Vendr: Home`,url:`https://www.vendr.com/`},{id:`vendr-news`,label:`IT Brief: Vertice buys Vendr`,url:`https://itbrief.news/story/vertice-buys-vendr-to-build-bigger-procurement-dataset`},{id:`vendr-aws`,label:`AWS Marketplace: Vendr listing`,url:`https://aws.amazon.com/marketplace/pp/prodview-lnfw3az34qe3c`}]},{slug:`zylo`,name:`Zylo`,title:`Truehound vs Zylo`,description:`Zylo tracks SaaS and AI spend for enterprise teams. Truehound audits who still has access and proves removal. A sourced, fair comparison.`,lede:`Zylo is a system of record for SaaS spend, built for larger IT, procurement and finance teams. Truehound is a small-team access audit. The overlap is narrow.`,shortAnswer:`Choose Zylo to see and control what you spend on SaaS and AI, starting from payments. Choose Truehound to find access that should be gone and remove it with proof. Zylo's access features run through Okta or Entra actions on its Premium plan.`,theyAreStronger:[`Spend-first discovery: it starts from real payments and shows licence waste, redundancy and renewals.`,`A large dataset for benchmarks, cost allocation and AI consumption tracking.`,`A chat assistant, Clarity, that plans work and asks for approval before consequential changes, plus an MCP server and API.`,`Fit for enterprise: multiple business units, M&A support and a managed service.`],weAreDifferent:[`It audits access itself, in the app, rather than acting through an identity provider.`,`Every removal is read back, and anything still present shows in red.`,`A published flat price. Zylo is quote-only, and its AWS Marketplace entry starts at $50,000 for 12 months.`],rows:[{topic:`Price`,them:`Quote only. Its AWS Marketplace listing shows a SaaS management platform at $50,000 for 12 months plus usage overage.`,themSources:[`zylo-pricing`,`zylo-aws`],us:s,usStatus:`live`},{topic:`What it is for`,them:`SaaS and AI spend: discovery, usage, renewals and cost allocation.`,themSources:[`zylo-product`],us:`Access: who has it, and proof the extra access is gone.`,usStatus:`live`},{topic:`Acting on access`,them:`Actions for Okta or Entra on Premium, and Jira automations.`,themSources:[`zylo-product`],us:`Removal inside the app after your approval, then a re-read. Live for GitHub today; others as connectors switch on.`,usStatus:`partial`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Spend and licence management`,them:`Yes. It is the core of the product.`,themSources:[`zylo-product`],us:`No. Truehound does not track spend.`,usStatus:`no`},{topic:`Getting started`,them:`Sales-led; we found no trial.`,themSources:[`zylo-pricing`],us:`Self-serve: run the free scan, then pay by card for names and fixes.`,usStatus:`live`}],useBoth:`Zylo for the spend picture and renewals. Truehound for the access side, where you need removals approved and proved.`,faq:[{q:`Does Zylo review who has access?`,a:`Its product page lists actions for Okta or Entra on Premium, which is access control through your identity provider. It does not describe an access review with evidence.`},{q:`Is Truehound a SaaS management tool?`,a:`No. It does not track spend, contracts or licences.`}],sources:[{id:`zylo-product`,label:`Zylo: Product`,url:`https://zylo.com/product`},{id:`zylo-pricing`,label:`Zylo: Pricing`,url:`https://zylo.com/pricing`},{id:`zylo-aws`,label:`AWS Marketplace: Zylo listing`,url:`https://aws.amazon.com/marketplace/pp/prodview-amjjpn3byw55c`}]},{slug:`yeshid`,name:`YeshID`,title:`Truehound vs YeshID`,description:`YeshID runs identity lifecycle and access reviews for Google Workspace teams. Truehound starts from GitHub and proves each removal. A sourced, fair comparison.`,lede:`YeshID is a capable identity tool for small teams, with a $0 tier for under 20 users. Truehound is narrower: an audit and removal of access, with proof.`,shortAnswer:`Choose YeshID if you want onboarding, offboarding, Slack access requests and reviews in one identity tool, especially on Google Workspace. Choose Truehound if you want a flat price that does not grow with headcount, GitHub depth, and a re-read after every removal.`,theyAreStronger:[`A full identity lifecycle: joiners, movers, leavers, workflows, access requests with time-based access and an app launcher.`,`Access reviews on real in-app roles, with direct remediation for automated apps and justification required.`,`A $0 tier for teams under 20 users and a trial of paid plans.`,`An assistant, Rae, that drafts workflows and policies and builds connector code.`,`Shadow IT and OAuth visibility with instant token revoke. Truehound does not do this today.`],weAreDifferent:[`One flat price rather than per user.`,`Findings start from GitHub: outside collaborators, deploy keys, webhooks and secrets in code.`,`Each removal is read back and unconfirmed ones stay red, so a closed task never stands for a removed account.`],rows:[{topic:`Price`,them:`$0 under 20 users. Growth is $5 a user a month in one table on its page and $9 billed annually in another; Business is $8 in one and custom in the other. We cannot tell which is current.`,themSources:[`yeshid-pricing`],us:s,usStatus:`live`},{topic:`Access reviews`,them:`Reviews on real roles and permissions, one model across integrated and manual apps, with escalation for sensitive decisions.`,themSources:[`yeshid-reviews`],us:`Snapshot, decisions, named sign-off and an evidence pack.`,usStatus:`coming`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Removing access`,them:`Direct remediation for automated apps. Many apps stay manual where a connection needs SCIM or REST and the right plan of the app.`,themSources:[`yeshid-reviews`,`yeshid-pricing`],us:`Removed after the approvals you set, then read again. Live for GitHub today; other apps as each connector switches on.`,usStatus:`partial`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Connectors`,them:`SCIM, REST and generated connector code, with CSV or screenshot import for the rest.`,themSources:[`yeshid-home`,`yeshid-pricing`],us:`GitHub today. Google Workspace, Entra, Slack and AWS IAM are built and switching on.`,usStatus:`partial`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Onboarding and access requests`,them:`Yes, through Slack and Teams, with approvals.`,themSources:[`yeshid-home`],us:`No. Truehound audits and removes; it does not provision.`,usStatus:`no`}],faq:[{q:`Which is cheaper?`,a:`Under 20 people YeshID's $0 tier wins. Above that, its per-user price grows with headcount while ours stays flat, so the answer depends on your size and which of its two published tables applies.`},{q:`Does YeshID prove removals?`,a:`It lists remediation for automated apps and request outcomes such as approved but not completed. Truehound adds a read of the app after each removal.`}],sources:[{id:`yeshid-home`,label:`YeshID: Home`,url:`https://www.yeshid.com/`},{id:`yeshid-pricing`,label:`YeshID: Pricing`,url:`https://www.yeshid.com/pricing`},{id:`yeshid-reviews`,label:`YeshID: Identity governance and access reviews`,url:`https://www.yeshid.com/solutions/identity-governance-access-reviews`}]}],l=[...i,...o,...c],u=`2026-10-22`,d=`{{price}}`,f=[{slug:`vanta`,name:`Vanta`,title:`Truehound vs Vanta`,description:`Vanta runs your compliance program. Truehound takes back the access it flags and proves it. A sourced, fair comparison, and when to use both.`,lede:`Vanta runs your compliance program. Truehound takes back the access that program flags, and proves it is gone. Most teams that need both should keep Vanta.`,shortAnswer:`Choose Vanta to run SOC 2 end to end: policies, controls, frameworks, the auditor relationship. Vanta's offboarding now removes accounts itself in six apps (Calendly, Zoom, Figma, Dropbox, Duo and Tailscale), and checks those removals; for GitHub, Slack, Google and AWS it still assigns tasks, and denying a user in an access review starts a remediation flow rather than removing access. Choose Truehound for those: it removes access in the apps it connects, with your approval, and reads each app again to prove every removal held.`,theyAreStronger:[`A whole compliance platform: frameworks, policies, control monitoring, vendor risk and a trust center. Truehound does none of that and does not try to.`,`Hundreds of integrations (Vanta lists more than 400). Truehound connects far fewer apps, and each connector card says exactly what it can read and remove.`,`Recurring access reviews you can schedule today, with reminders and a login for your auditor.`,`Automatic removal during offboarding in six apps (Calendly, Zoom, Figma, Dropbox, Duo and Tailscale), with completion checked for integrated systems.`,`AI recommendations on access review rows, and last login for Google and Entra users.`,`Offboarding tasks that record whether each removal was confirmed by an integration or by a person.`,`Years in market and a large auditor network. Truehound is new.`],weAreDifferent:[`It removes access where Vanta opens a task: GitHub members, outside collaborators and keys today, with Slack, Google and AWS as connectors switch on, under the approval policy you set.`,`After removing, it reads the app again and shows anything still present in red, never as done.`,`The price is on the website, flat, monthly, cancel in the product. Vanta prices by quote after a demo.`,`GitHub depth: outside collaborators, deploy keys, webhooks, public repos and secrets in code are their own findings, not one account row.`],rows:[{topic:`Price`,them:`Not published; every plan is quoted after a demo. Its AWS Marketplace listing shows 12-month packages from $6,000 to $23,000.`,themSources:[`vanta-pricing`,`vanta-aws`],us:d,usStatus:`live`},{topic:`Access reviews`,them:`Part of Access Management, which Vanta's help says may require an upgrade or add-on.`,themSources:[`vanta-uar`],us:`Every paid review plan: snapshot, decisions, named sign-off and an evidence pack.`,usStatus:`coming`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`When a reviewer denies access`,them:`The review does not remove access: denying a user starts a remediation flow. Separately, offboarding removes accounts automatically in six apps (Calendly, Zoom, Figma, Dropbox, Duo and Tailscale); elsewhere, including GitHub, Slack, Google and AWS, it creates tasks.`,themSources:[`vanta-uar`,`vanta-releases`],us:`Removed from the row, after the approvals you set. Live for GitHub today; other apps as each connector switches on.`,usStatus:`partial`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Proof the removal happened`,them:`Checks completion automatically for integrated systems and records whether each removal was confirmed through an integration or by hand.`,themSources:[`vanta-offboarding`,`vanta-releases`],us:`Re-reads every connected app after every removal and keeps the before and after; manual removals are labelled manual.`,usStatus:`coming`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Knowing who left`,them:`From your HR system or identity provider; when both are connected, HR status wins.`,themSources:[`vanta-personnel`],us:`You tell it who left today (GitHub). With Google Workspace or Entra connected, a suspension starts the check across every connected app.`,usStatus:`partial`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Integrations`,them:`More than 400 listed.`,themSources:[`vanta-integrations`],us:`GitHub today. Google Workspace, Entra, Slack, AWS IAM and more are built and switching on.`,usStatus:`partial`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Getting started`,them:`Sales-led: a demo, then onboarding.`,themSources:[`vanta-pricing`],us:`Self-serve: run the free scan in minutes and pay by card when you want names and fixes.`,usStatus:`live`},{topic:`Compliance program (policies, controls, frameworks, auditor portal)`,them:`Yes. It is the core of the product.`,themSources:[`vanta-pricing`],us:`No. Truehound is not a compliance platform and holds no certification of its own.`,usStatus:`no`}],useBoth:`Keep Vanta as the system of record for SOC 2. Run the access review and the removals in Truehound, then upload its evidence files to Vanta as the evidence for your access review control. Vanta tracks. Truehound fixes.`,faq:[{q:`Does Truehound replace Vanta?`,a:`No. Truehound does not run a compliance program, manage policies or work with your auditor. It finds access that should not exist, removes it with your approval, and hands you proof.`},{q:`Does Vanta remove access when a reviewer denies it?`,a:`Not through the review: per its help center, denying a user starts a remediation flow. Its offboarding does remove accounts automatically in six apps (Calendly, Zoom, Figma, Dropbox, Duo and Tailscale, per its release notes as of the date below); for other apps it assigns tasks.`},{q:`Can I upload Truehound's evidence into Vanta?`,a:`That is what the evidence pack is built for: one CSV per app plus a manifest, laid out the way auditors ask. It ships with the access review plans.`}],sources:[{id:`vanta-pricing`,label:`Vanta: Pricing`,url:`https://www.vanta.com/pricing`},{id:`vanta-uar`,label:`Vanta Help Center: Access reviews`,url:`https://help.vanta.com/en/articles/11345416-access-reviews`},{id:`vanta-offboarding`,label:`Vanta Help Center: Offboarding personnel`,url:`https://help.vanta.com/en/articles/11345609-offboarding-personnel`},{id:`vanta-personnel`,label:`Vanta Help Center: Adding and managing personnel`,url:`https://help.vanta.com/en/articles/11345379-adding-and-managing-personnel`},{id:`vanta-integrations`,label:`Vanta: Integrations`,url:`https://www.vanta.com/integrations`},{id:`vanta-aws`,label:`AWS Marketplace: Vanta listing`,url:`https://aws.amazon.com/marketplace/pp/prodview-5ophamrbfxt44`},{id:`vanta-releases`,label:`Vanta Help Center: Product updates (release notes)`,url:`https://help.vanta.com/en/articles/11345422-product-updates`}]},{slug:`drata`,name:`Drata`,title:`Truehound vs Drata`,description:`Drata reviews access for your audit. Truehound removes what fails the review and proves it. A sourced, fair comparison, and when to use both.`,lede:`Drata runs your compliance program and your access review evidence. Truehound removes the access that fails the review, and checks again that it is gone.`,shortAnswer:`Choose Drata to automate SOC 2 and other frameworks end to end. Choose Truehound for the removing: Drata's help center says its access review is focused on review and does not include remediation. Truehound removes access with your approval and keeps the proof.`,theyAreStronger:[`A full compliance automation platform across many frameworks, with continuous control monitoring and an auditor workflow.`,`Typed warnings in the review (former personnel, missing MFA, unlinked users, service accounts, admins) that double as filters.`,`Evidence attached automatically to the access review control, with a renewal date.`,`Hundreds of integrations and a Jira automation for offboarding evidence.`],weAreDifferent:[`It removes access from the finding, under your approval policy, instead of leaving removal to you.`,`The review starts from a frozen snapshot, so the evidence shows access as of the review date.`,`Reviews are included in every review plan, not an add-on, and the price is public.`,`Proof is a re-read of the app, not an uploaded file or a ticket link.`],rows:[{topic:`Price`,them:`Not published: Drata no longer has a public pricing page, and plans are quoted after a demo.`,themSources:[`drata-pricing`],us:d,usStatus:`live`},{topic:`Access reviews`,them:`An add-on for Foundation and Advanced, included with Enterprise, enabled by contacting support.`,themSources:[`drata-uar`],us:`Included in every review plan.`,usStatus:`coming`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Removing access that fails review`,them:`Not included: Drata says its solution is focused on review and does not include remediation.`,themSources:[`drata-uar`],us:`Removed from the row, after the approvals you set. Live for GitHub today; other apps as each connector switches on.`,usStatus:`partial`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Point-in-time snapshot`,them:`No snapshot at the start; reviewers see access as it is on the day they review.`,themSources:[`drata-uar`],us:`Frozen at launch, with each app's read time in the evidence.`,usStatus:`coming`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Reviews running at once`,them:`One active review period at a time, one reviewer per application.`,themSources:[`drata-uar`],us:`Reviewers per app or per manager; the next quarter is scheduled when one finishes.`,usStatus:`coming`,usDep:`backend`},{topic:`Offboarding proof`,them:`Upload a file or a ticket link, or connect a Jira automation; Drata does not remove the accounts.`,themSources:[`drata-offboarding`],us:`A re-read of each app after removal, labelled verified, manual with attachment, or still present.`,usStatus:`coming`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Compliance program (frameworks, controls, auditor workflow)`,them:`Yes. It is the core of the product.`,themSources:[`drata-pricing`],us:`No. Truehound is not a compliance platform and holds no certification of its own.`,usStatus:`no`}],useBoth:`Keep Drata for SOC 2. Run the review and the removals in Truehound, then attach its evidence files to your access review control in Drata.`,faq:[{q:`Does Drata remove access after an access review?`,a:`Not according to its own help center, checked on the date below: the access review is focused on review and does not include remediation.`},{q:`Is Drata's access review included in every plan?`,a:`Drata's help center says User Access Review is an add-on for Foundation and Advanced and included with Enterprise.`},{q:`Does Truehound replace Drata?`,a:`No. Use Drata to run the compliance program; use Truehound to do the removing and produce the proof.`}],sources:[{id:`drata-pricing`,label:`Drata: website (the old /pricing page now shows the home page)`,url:`https://drata.com/`},{id:`drata-uar`,label:`Drata Help Center: Run an access review`,url:`https://help.drata.com/en/articles/13557396-run-an-access-review`},{id:`drata-offboarding`,label:`Drata Help Center: Personnel offboarding evidence`,url:`https://help.drata.com/en/articles/13613987-personnel-offboarding-evidence-new-experience`}]},{slug:`nudge`,name:`Nudge Security`,title:`Truehound vs Nudge Security`,description:`Nudge Security discovers every SaaS app and nudges owners to act. Truehound removes access through each app's API and proves it. A sourced, fair comparison.`,lede:`Nudge Security is excellent at finding the apps nobody told IT about. Truehound is narrower: it works on the apps you connect, and removes access itself.`,shortAnswer:`Choose Nudge if your biggest problem is not knowing which SaaS apps people signed up for. Choose Truehound if you know your apps and need leavers removed, with proof, on a monthly price. For apps outside single sign-on, Nudge asks the app's owner to remove the account; Truehound removes it through the app's API where one exists.`,theyAreStronger:[`Discovery: Nudge finds apps from signup emails, OAuth grants and a browser extension. Truehound does not read email and has no browser extension.`,`A mature offboarding playbook that locks the person out of Google or Microsoft, transfers critical resources and revokes OAuth grants.`,`A trial with no sales call and no payment details, which Truehound does not offer (we offer the free scan and the live demo instead).`,`Flat pricing up to 150 users, with every feature in every tier.`],weAreDifferent:[`It removes access through each app's API where one exists, instead of asking someone else to.`,`It reads the app again after removing, so the proof is the app's own answer.`,`Monthly billing, cancel in the product. Nudge's published plans are billed annually.`,`GitHub depth: deploy keys, outside collaborators, webhooks, public repos and secrets in code.`],rows:[{topic:`Price`,them:`$750 a month for up to 150 users, then $5 a user a month to 1,500, billed annually, all features included.`,themSources:[`nudge-pricing`],us:d,usStatus:`live`},{topic:`Trying it`,them:`A trial that asks for no payment details and no sales call.`,themSources:[`nudge-trial`],us:`The free scan on your own GitHub org, with names hidden, and the live demo. Paid plans unlock names and fixes.`,usStatus:`live`},{topic:`Finding apps nobody connected`,them:`Yes: signup emails, OAuth grants, browser extension and login logs.`,themSources:[`nudge-faq`],us:`Narrower: the apps you connect, plus OAuth grants once Google Workspace is connected.`,usStatus:`coming`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Removing an account outside single sign-on`,them:`Nudges the app's technical owner with instructions and a confirm link, and tracks the answer.`,themSources:[`nudge-uar`,`nudge-offboarding`],us:`Removes it through the app's API where one exists, after your approvals; a deep link and a manual label where none does.`,usStatus:`partial`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Access review output`,them:`A printable PDF of apps in scope and the users verified or removed.`,themSources:[`nudge-uar`],us:`An evidence pack: one CSV per app, decisions, revokes, leavers, a not-checked list and file hashes.`,usStatus:`coming`,usDep:`sources`}],faq:[{q:`Is Truehound a SaaS discovery tool?`,a:`Not mainly. Nudge Security is stronger at discovery. Truehound focuses on removing access from the apps you connect and proving it.`},{q:`Can I pay monthly for Nudge Security?`,a:`Its pricing page lists the Essential and Growth plans as billed annually, checked on the date below.`}],sources:[{id:`nudge-pricing`,label:`Nudge Security: Pricing`,url:`https://www.nudgesecurity.com/pricing`},{id:`nudge-trial`,label:`Nudge Security: Trial`,url:`https://www.nudgesecurity.com/trial`},{id:`nudge-faq`,label:`Nudge Security: FAQ`,url:`https://nudgesecurity.com/faqs`},{id:`nudge-uar`,label:`Nudge Security blog: Streamline SOC 2 access reviews`,url:`https://www.nudgesecurity.com/post/streamline-soc-2-access-reviews-with-nudge-security`},{id:`nudge-offboarding`,label:`Nudge Security blog: Streamline employee offboarding`,url:`https://www.nudgesecurity.com/post/streamline-employee-offboarding-with-nudge-security`}]},{slug:`accessowl`,name:`AccessOwl`,title:`Truehound vs AccessOwl`,description:`AccessOwl runs access requests and offboarding in Slack. Truehound starts from who left and proves removal. A sourced, fair comparison.`,lede:`AccessOwl is a strong Slack-first access tool with public pricing. Truehound is flat-priced, starts from who left in your identity provider, and checks every removal again.`,shortAnswer:`Choose AccessOwl if you want access requests, approvals and onboarding run inside Slack across hundreds of apps. Choose Truehound if you want a flat price, reviews that schedule themselves, and offboarding that starts when someone is suspended in Google or Entra.`,theyAreStronger:[`Access requests and approvals, onboarding and provisioning, not only offboarding.`,`A long list of integrations, including ones driven by a service account for apps without an API.`,`Slack-native: approvals, reminders and tasks happen where the team already works.`,`Completed reviews sync to Vanta automatically.`,`More integrations today: AccessOwl lists more than 400. Truehound connects far fewer, and says which.`,`Its trust center lists SOC 2. Truehound holds no SOC 2 report.`,`Public pricing and a self-serve trial.`],weAreDifferent:[`Flat price, never per seat. AccessOwl charges per user with a monthly minimum.`,`Quarterly reviews on a schedule; AccessOwl's docs say every review is started by hand.`,`A Google or Entra suspension starts offboarding; in AccessOwl it marks the user inactive.`,`Removals are checked by reading the app again, so nothing is closed as removed while it still exists.`],rows:[{topic:`Price`,them:`$4.50 or $6.00 a user a month, $250 minimum; access reviews need the $6.00 Growth plan, and automated provisioning is a $2.50 add-on. Their own Okta comparison prices 100 employees at $8.50 a user, $10,200 a year.`,themSources:[`ao-pricing`,`ao-okta`],us:d,usStatus:`live`},{topic:`Recurring reviews`,them:`Not today: every campaign is started by hand, and a saved review does not pick up apps added later.`,themSources:[`ao-uar`],us:`Quarterly by default; the next review is scheduled when one finishes, with newly connected apps included.`,usStatus:`coming`,usDep:`backend`},{topic:`Suspension in Google or Microsoft`,them:`Marks the user inactive; assigned applications are not removed by default. Offboarding starts from Slack, the admin page or an HR integration.`,themSources:[`ao-offboarding`],us:`A suspension is the trigger: every connected app is checked for that person and removals are proposed for approval.`,usStatus:`coming`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Apps without an integration`,them:`A Slack task to the app's admin; Mark as removed closes the record in AccessOwl without removing anything in the app.`,themSources:[`ao-offboarding`],us:`A deep link and a manual label; the next read shows if the account is still there, in red.`,usStatus:`coming`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Where it runs`,them:`Slack is the main interface: installing the Slack app starts the trial.`,themSources:[`ao-trial`],us:`In the browser, with email. Slack is optional, never required.`,usStatus:`live`},{topic:`Vanta sync`,them:`Completed reviews sync to Vanta automatically.`,themSources:[`ao-uar`],us:`Evidence files built to upload into Vanta or Drata; no automatic sync.`,usStatus:`coming`,usDep:`sources`}],faq:[{q:`Does AccessOwl schedule recurring access reviews?`,a:`Its docs say not today: each campaign is started manually, and they suggest setting your own reminder.`},{q:`Which is cheaper?`,a:`It depends on headcount. AccessOwl's access reviews cost $6 a user a month with a $250 minimum, so the bill grows with your team. Truehound is one flat price.`}],cost:{plan:`AccessOwl Growth (access reviews) with the provisioning add-on`,perUserMonthly:8.5,minimumMonthly:250,sources:[`ao-pricing`,`ao-okta`]},sources:[{id:`ao-pricing`,label:`AccessOwl: Pricing`,url:`https://www.accessowl.com/pricing`},{id:`ao-uar`,label:`AccessOwl docs: Access reviews`,url:`https://docs.accessowl.com/guides/access-reviews.md`},{id:`ao-offboarding`,label:`AccessOwl docs: Offboarding`,url:`https://docs.accessowl.com/guides/onboarding-offboarding/offboarding.md`},{id:`ao-okta`,label:`AccessOwl: Alternative to Okta (cost model at 100 employees)`,url:`https://www.accessowl.com/alternative-to-okta`},{id:`ao-trust`,label:`AccessOwl: Trust center`,url:`https://security.accessowl.com`},{id:`ao-trial`,label:`AccessOwl docs: Trial kickoff`,url:`https://docs.accessowl.com/general/trial-kickoff.md`}]},{slug:`okta`,name:`Okta`,title:`Truehound vs Okta`,description:`Okta is an identity platform for SSO, MFA and lifecycle. Truehound keeps Google or Entra as your core and proves access is gone. Sourced, with costs.`,lede:`Okta is an identity platform: single sign-on, MFA and lifecycle management. Truehound is not one. It keeps the Google Workspace or Entra directory you already pay for as your identity core, and checks, removes and proves access in the apps around it.`,shortAnswer:`Choose Okta when you need enterprise single sign-on, adaptive MFA and automated provisioning across many apps, and have someone to run it. Choose Truehound when Google Workspace or Entra is already your directory, you are 10 to 200 people, and the job is catching leavers in every app, removing their access with approval, and proving it to an auditor, at a flat price instead of per seat.`,theyAreStronger:[`Single sign-on and MFA for your whole company, with adaptive policies. Truehound offers neither.`,`Automated provisioning and deprovisioning through Lifecycle Management for apps it is connected to.`,`A very large prebuilt integration network and years of enterprise use.`,`A dedicated identity team can do far more with it: device trust, complex group rules, many directories.`],weAreDifferent:[`Flat price, never per seat, monthly. Okta is per user, billed annually, with an annual minimum.`,`No new identity provider: Google Workspace or Entra stays the source of who works here.`,`It looks at the access that never passes through a login: GitHub outside collaborators, deploy keys, secrets in code.`,`After a removal it reads the app again and keeps the result as evidence, labelled verified, manual or still present.`],rows:[{topic:`Price`,them:`Essentials, which includes Lifecycle Management, is listed at $17 a user a month; Starter is $6 with Lifecycle Management as an add-on.`,themSources:[`okta-pricing`],us:d,usStatus:`live`},{topic:`Billing`,them:`Billed annually, with a $1,500 annual contract minimum for Workforce Identity.`,themSources:[`okta-pricing`],us:`Monthly or once; cancel in the product.`,usStatus:`live`},{topic:`Single sign-on and MFA`,them:`Yes. It is the core of the product.`,themSources:[`okta-pricing`],us:`No. Truehound does not replace your login; Google Workspace or Entra does that.`,usStatus:`no`},{topic:`Removing access when someone leaves`,them:`Deactivating a user deprovisions their accounts in apps set up for provisioning with deactivation. Apps not provisioned through Okta are not touched.`,themSources:[`okta-lcm`,`okta-deactivate`],us:`Finds the person in each connected app and removes access through the app's own API after your approvals, with a labelled manual step where there is no API. Live for GitHub today.`,usStatus:`partial`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`The SSO tax`,them:`Single sign-on and SCIM for an app often need that app's higher plan. sso.tax lists, for example, Figma at $12 a user a month on its base plan and $45 with SSO.`,themSources:[`sso-tax`],us:`Uses each app's admin API on the plan you have where it exists, so a removal and its record do not wait for an enterprise upgrade.`,usStatus:`coming`,usDep:`sources`}],useBoth:`Already on Okta? Keep it for login. Truehound still checks what Okta does not provision: GitHub outside collaborators, deploy keys, cloud access keys and apps people signed up for with a password.`,faq:[{q:`Can I skip Okta at 10 to 200 people?`,a:`Many companies do: Google Workspace or Entra can be the directory and the login for important apps. See the lean access management playbook at /guides/lean-access-management-google-workspace-entra.`},{q:`Does Truehound do single sign-on?`,a:`No. Your login stays with Google Workspace or Entra. Truehound handles who still has access, removal and proof.`}],cost:{plan:`Okta Essentials (includes Lifecycle Management)`,perUserMonthly:17,minimumAnnual:1500,sources:[`okta-pricing`],excludes:`Upgrades to SSO or SCIM plans in your other apps (the SSO tax), which Okta needs to provision them, are not included.`},sources:[{id:`okta-pricing`,label:`Okta: Workforce Identity pricing`,url:`https://www.okta.com/pricing/`},{id:`okta-lcm`,label:`Okta Help: Automatically deactivate app users`,url:`https://help.okta.com/en-us/Content/Topics/provisioning/lcm/lcm-automatic-deactivate.htm`},{id:`okta-deactivate`,label:`Okta Help: Deactivate and delete user accounts`,url:`https://help.okta.com/en-us/content/topics/users-groups-profiles/usgp-deactivate-user-account.htm`},{id:`sso-tax`,label:`sso.tax: The SSO Wall of Shame`,url:`https://sso.tax/`}]},{slug:`spreadsheets`,name:`a spreadsheet`,title:`Truehound vs a spreadsheet access review`,description:`Auditors accept spreadsheet access reviews. Here is what filling one really takes, and when a tool is worth it. Sourced and fair.`,lede:`A spreadsheet is a perfectly good access review. Auditors accept it. The work is in filling it, and in proving the removals happened.`,shortAnswer:`Stay on a spreadsheet if you have a handful of apps, few leavers and the hours to spare: use our free template and you are in good shape. Use Truehound when pulling every user list, matching people across apps and proving each removal takes more of your quarter than it should.`,theyAreStronger:[`It costs nothing and you already know how to use it.`,`Auditors accept spreadsheet evidence for access reviews.`,`Total flexibility: any app, any column, any reviewer.`,`No vendor gets access to your systems.`],weAreDifferent:[`The user list of each app is read from its API with the read time recorded, not exported or screenshotted.`,`Leavers are matched across apps for you, and arrive pre-marked for removal.`,`Removal happens from the same screen, and is checked again.`,`The next quarter is scheduled before you forget it.`],rows:[{topic:`Cost`,them:`Nothing, plus the hours each quarter.`,themSources:[`thoropass`],us:d,usStatus:`live`},{topic:`Getting each app's user list`,them:`Export or screenshot each admin page by hand.`,themSources:[`iden`],us:`Read from the app's API, with the time of the read. GitHub today, more apps as connectors switch on.`,usStatus:`partial`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Finding people who left`,them:`Cross-check the HR list against every app by eye.`,themSources:[`iden`],us:`Matched by email to Google Workspace or Entra; accounts with no match get their own list.`,usStatus:`coming`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Removing access`,them:`A separate step in each app's admin page.`,themSources:[`iden`],us:`From the finding, after your approvals. Live for GitHub.`,usStatus:`partial`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Proof it was removed`,them:`Usually a screenshot, if anyone remembers.`,themSources:[`iden`],us:`A re-read of the app, kept with the decision.`,usStatus:`coming`,usDep:`sources`},{topic:`Not missing a quarter`,them:`Nothing reminds you. A missed quarterly review is a commonly listed audit exception.`,themSources:[`bastion`],us:`Scheduled reviews with reminders to each reviewer.`,usStatus:`coming`,usDep:`backend`}],faq:[{q:`Will my auditor accept a spreadsheet?`,a:`Generally, yes. Auditors ask for the evidence of the review (who had access, who decided, when, and what changed), not for a particular tool.`},{q:`How often do access reviews cause audit problems?`,a:`In a benchmark of 193 SOC reports, access reviews caused about 15.6% of exceptions and terminations about 12%.`}],sources:[{id:`thoropass`,label:`Thoropass: Evidence of access review documentation`,url:`https://help.thoropass.com/en/articles/9184313-er-50-evidence-of-access-review-documentation-for-a-sample-of-quarters-months`},{id:`iden`,label:`Iden: Identity evidence playbook for ISO 27001 and SOC 2`,url:`https://www.idenhq.com/identity-evidence-playbook-iso-27001-soc-2`},{id:`bastion`,label:`Bastion: Common SOC 2 exceptions`,url:`https://bastion.tech/learn/soc2/common-exceptions/`},{id:`cbiz`,label:`CBIZ: 2024 SOC benchmark study, part three`,url:`https://www.cbiz.com/insights/article/the-evolution-of-soc-reporting-final-insights-from-the-2024-soc-benchmark-study-part-three`}]},...l];export{f as n,d as r,u as t};